# Datalog in the mixin encoding of inheritance
# (corpora/contest_mixin_library.pl): scored points of the hard corpus,
# ported from the retired labeled points of contest_inheritance_datalog.pl
# and contest_inheritance_datalog_second.pl, which encoded the MIXINv2
# paper's appendix "Encoding Datalog in Inheritance-Calculus" with tries
# walked by label. Each asks whether a tuple is in a relation, boolean(true);
# a tuple has as many answers as derivations and a tuple outside the
# relation has none, so these points are asked by membership, as the reachability points of contest_mixin_claims.pl are.
#
# THE ENCODING. A program is a module whose slots are its constants'
# classes and its relations' classes.
# - The class of constant c implements c's marker is_c alone, the Church
#   true, so that a union of constant classes answers is_c once for each
#   c in the union and nothing when c is not in it. It also implements
#   accept, \relation. relation.c: a constant reads its own row off a
#   relation, the visitor dispatch of the labeled port's acceptance.
# - A relation's hierarchy has one slot per constant: R.x is the union of
#   the classes of the y with R(x, y). A fact R(x, y) is a mixin of R's
#   class that defines x as y's class, and a rule with head R is a mixin
#   of R's class that defines every x, so that R's class, the union of
#   its facts and rules, answers the union of what each derives: the
#   deep merge is Datalog's union, and a recursive rule reads its own head
#   relation through the module's self.
# - the rules, for each constant x:
#   - join, H(x, z) :- R1(x, y1), R2(y1, y2), ..., Rn(y(n-1), z):
#     H.x = (... (R1.x.accept R2).accept ... Rn), each step dispatching
#     from a union of constants to the union of their rows;
#   - intersection, H(x, y) :- L(x, y), R(x, y): H.x is the union over
#     the constants c of the guard "c in L.x" of the guard "c in R.x" of
#     c's class, where the guard "c in S" of T is S.is_c T [];
#   - constant join, H(x, w) :- R(x, y), R(y, k), R(k, w): H.x is the
#     guard "k in (R.x.accept R)" of R.k;
#   - swap, H(x, y) :- B(y, x): H.x is the union over the constants y of
#     the guard "x in B.y" of y's class.
# - The test reads R.first.is_second.
#
# THE PROGRAMS are the labeled family's, over its constants and facts:
# composition, the three-way join, the intersection, the constant join,
# the transitive closure of RelationalCycle over rings, a chain and a
# line, and the symmetric transitive closure. Each posed tuple is in the
# least fixed point that reference_holds/4 computes in Prolog by naive
# iteration, and every point's term checks it.
#
# PROOF of each scored claim, a finite head: the tuple's finite derivation
# (datalog_point/6) is a finite sequence of choices, one element of a
# union at each rule application and at each dispatch of a join, that
# reaches second's marker in first's row of the relation, the Church true,
# whose test yields \a. a; and lambda beta eta with sets is consistent.
#
# LIMITS: the family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - semi-naive evaluation of the program's least fixpoint: F. Bancilhon, R.
#   Ramakrishnan, An Amateur's Introduction to Recursive Query Processing
#   Strategies, SIGMOD 1986, doi:10.1145/16856.16859.
# - tabled evaluation: W. Chen, D. S. Warren, Tabled Evaluation with
#   Delaying for General Logic Programs, J. ACM 1996,
#   doi:10.1145/227595.227597.

#   ---- the points ----
#   datalog_point(?Point, ?Program, ?Relation, ?First, ?Second, ?Derivation)
#
# The program is a constructor of mixin_datalog/program, closure and
# symmetric applied to a constructor of mixin_datalog/shape, ring and line
# applied to their size. Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal,
# a Scott list of bytes, imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
